WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.845 --> 00:00:09.845
(baby cooing)
(water trickling)

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:19.300
- [Woman] My mind kept
repeating a single thought:

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.067
He had taken from me my innocence,

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.503
my youth, my heart.

00:00:24.503 --> 00:00:25.420
Everything.

00:00:27.097 --> 00:00:29.930
(water splashing)

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.403
So I took from him the only thing I could.

00:00:58.076 --> 00:01:01.243
(Ying-Ying screaming)

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.870
- Really, nobody's
gonna crack open a book?

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.470
- They're in there.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.780
- Why do they lock this?
- So we don't steal them.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:11.900
- You're here for the engineering club?

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.300
- I'd like to enter this.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.230
- [Woman] The fifth annual
Underwater Robotics Competition.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.640
- So you wanna go up against

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.520
the best tech schools in the country?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.070
- Says they scout for
internships, and jobs.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.070
- You guys got some ground to make up.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.890
The other teams will
have more money than you.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.340
- So you're still short $432.54.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.723
- And my man ate a couple
ice creams on the way in.

00:01:31.723 --> 00:01:33.220
- Can I have an ice cream?

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.590
- Too late.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.090
I already wrote the check.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.510
Okay, we gotta find cheap,

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.389
creative ways to build this thing.

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:40.388
(zapping)
(student screams)

00:01:40.388 --> 00:01:42.490
- How do we fix it?
- We gotta stop this leak.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.340
- [Fredi] Small and really absorbent.

00:01:46.752 --> 00:01:49.002
(cheering)

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.460
- Next case.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.530
- [Clerk] City of Atlanta
versus Mabel Simmons.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:55.917
- Hey, Cora.
- Hey.

00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:57.020
- Hey, Brown.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.410
If the Lord get me out of
this, I'm going by the church.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:01.270
- You better mean it, too.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.281
- I'm going by the church.
- You better mean it.

00:02:03.281 --> 00:02:04.440
- I mean it.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.280
Hey, Judge.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.070
Oh, girl, your hair is so pretty.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.590
Who doing your hair?

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.450
Lord have mercy.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.660
How you doing?

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.360
- Mabel Simmons, you come before me

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.040
more than you go to the
doctor for a checkup.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.210
- Listen, I done changed my life.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.789
You ain't got to say nothing.
I am living for the Lord.

00:02:17.789 --> 00:02:19.540
- Since when?
- I am living for the Lord.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.550
I am living for the Lord.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.590
- Mabel-
- Hallelujah.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.900
I feel him down in my spirit.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.470
You know, as I think about
the goodness of Jesus

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.180
and all that he has done for me,

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.950
my soul cries out, "Hallelujah,
thank God for saving me."

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.389
- [Mablean] You finished?

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:39.222
- Mm-hmm.

00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:52.585
- Hello, everyone,

00:02:52.585 --> 00:02:56.020
and welcome to the Academy's
Visiting Artists Film Series,

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.840
FilmAid Edition for Film Editing.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.640
I'm Dody Dorn here in Los Angeles.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.100
I'm a governor of the Academy
in the Editor's Branch.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.680
I have the distinct pleasure

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.340
of introducing Maysie Hoy,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.943
who I've known for over 40 years.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.843
(Maysie chuckles)
A long time.

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:17.030
(laughs) And I'm very proud and humbled

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.920
to be able to introduce her to you.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.743
The amazing Maysie Hoy.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:23.960
- Thank you, Dody.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.820
I mean, people don't
know that Dody herself

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.610
is a well-respected
editor in her own right.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.040
So she has a great body of work.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.950
So get onto the web and
check out her resume.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.150
Anyways, hi to everyone.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.710
My name is Maysie Hoy.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:49.150
I'm a film editor, and I
live in Los Angeles as well.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:53.940
And I guess, where do we start, Dody?

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.120
From the beginning?

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.600
- Yeah, first I just wanna thank
you so much for doing this.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.340
It really means a lot
to the students, I know,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.010
and other people out there
who are watching this.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.560
So it's really great that you're willing

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.170
to share your time and your experiences.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.290
And I would just love to know

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.663
how you first got started
in the film industry.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.080
- Well, it's...

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.660
I started out as an actress,
living in Vancouver.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.940
And at a very, very young age,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.500
this is very strange,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.900
I think as far back as I can remember,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.860
when I was like, four or five years old,

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.010
this is what I wanted to do.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.030
Which is crazy because where I grew up

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.320
with very traditional parents,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.300
being Chinese and very, very old school,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.850
that was the furthest
thing from their minds

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.560
of what I should be doing.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.190
And so with that in the back of my mind,

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.830
it just sort of...

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.000
To please my family, I told
them I wanted to be a nurse,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.297
but when I graduated I said,

00:05:01.297 --> 00:05:03.110
"Okay, I don't know how

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.050
I'm gonna get into the film business,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:09.200
but I'll go and study to be a dietician."

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.740
And then in my spare time
I could find acting work.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:17.740
And so one day I'm in the
cafeteria of this college

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.650
and I see all these students having fun.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.920
They were just having a great time.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.237
So I approached them and I said,

00:05:25.237 --> 00:05:27.720
"Can I ask you what
classes you guys are in,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.553
or what's your major?"

00:05:28.553 --> 00:05:29.600
And they go, "Theater arts."

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.730
I go, "Oh wow, that's great."
I said, "I didn't even know

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.390
they had a theater arts program here."

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.490
And I said, "Well, how do I get in it?"

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:38.820
And they said, "Well, first of all,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.710
you go to your counselor
and ask to be transferred."

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:42.780
I said, "Okay, great."

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.730
So I head off to the counselor's office.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:46.800
And you've gotta understand

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.640
that Vancouver is a very
provincial kind of city.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.490
In fact, very stoic, very English.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.740
So I walked into the counselor's office,

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:00.200
and there's a woman
sitting behind the desk,

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.930
gray hair with big bun.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.430
She was really, really nice.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.597
And I said to her, I said,

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:08.590
"Look, I wanna transfer out
of the dietician program

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:11.920
and get into the theater arts program."

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.957
And she kinda looked at me and said,

00:06:15.957 --> 00:06:18.430
"Well, are you sure
this is what you want?"

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:19.930
I says, "Yeah, this is something

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.070
I've dreamed of doing all my life."

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.930
And she goes, "You know, there's
not gonna be a lot of work

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.059
for somebody like you."

00:06:27.059 --> 00:06:28.523
And I look at her and I go,

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:31.010
"Well, it doesn't matter."

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:32.970
I said, "This is what I wanna do,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.283
so can you please sign the transfer

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.550
for me to get into the theater program?"

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:41.513
So she signed it,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.940
and off I went to the
basement of this college.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.240
And I walked in, and there was this very

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.790
English-looking gentleman
who ran the program.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.223
And it was Tony Holland.

00:06:57.006 --> 00:07:00.610
He had worked at the
Bristol Old Vic in London,

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.550
and now he was living in Vancouver

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.410
and he was head of this theater program.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:07.480
So I gave him the transfer note,

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.520
and he looked at the transfer note,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.840
he looked up at me a couple times,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.350
and he goes, "Why do you wanna do this?"

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.460
And I go, "Well, I wanna be an actress."

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.000
And he said basically the same thing.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.020
He goes, "You know,
there's not a lot of work

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:22.407
for somebody like you."

00:07:22.407 --> 00:07:24.527
And I looked at him, I go,

00:07:24.527 --> 00:07:26.443
"Just let me in the class.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.050
You don't even have to talk to me,

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:30.450
you don't have to bother me.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.957
Just let me in the class."

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:35.650
So he did.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.810
And it's interesting
because many years later

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:40.833
I became good friends with his wife.

00:07:42.474 --> 00:07:45.380
Her name was Gusta, and she said to me,

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.550
she says, "You know, he came home that day

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:48.860
and he said, 'Can you believe it?

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.600
There's this Chinese woman
that wants to be an actress.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.080
And besides, she's not
that pretty.'" (laughs)

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.740
And so anyways, I always
used this against him

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.590
because when I see him after I graduated

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:05.800
and came to Hollywood and
made a career of myself,

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.767
he would always look at
me sheepishly and say,

00:08:09.767 --> 00:08:14.170
"I'm really, really sorry
about what I said to you."

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.200
I said, "You know what?

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.200
What that did was it motivated
me to create my own program,

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.090
or find my own way."

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:23.070
So once again,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:28.070
I'm sitting in my friend's
apartment, and she said,

00:08:30.447 --> 00:08:34.320
"You know, they're casting
this movie in town."

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.300
I go...

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:37.320
Well, it's Robert Altman.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.610
He's doing a movie called
"McCabe and Mrs. Miller".

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.280
And so she says, "You know what?

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.630
You should see if you can get on that."

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.430
And so then at the time,
I met somebody who,

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:54.430
at that time, at a book of actors.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:55.763
And I got involved in that.

00:08:55.763 --> 00:08:57.360
You know, they take your picture,

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.670
they put it in this book

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.800
and they give it to the casting directors.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.610
And so one day I get this phone call

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:06.010
from somebody called Graham Clifford.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.750
Graham now is a director,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.090
but he was the casting director.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:14.320
So he brought me in, and
we sat and we talked a lot.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.700
And so then I sort of left it.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.330
And he goes, "Well,
Bob is gonna be in town

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:24.590
in about a month, so I'm
gonna keep you in mind,

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.090
so just stay in touch."

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:31.090
And so I get a phone call
from Robert Altman's office,

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:36.850
and Graham said, "Well,
Bob wants to meet you."

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.067
And so I said, "Okay."

00:09:40.396 --> 00:09:42.160
At that time I didn't have a car,

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.420
but I took a bus, which took about an hour

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.380
to get to where Bob was.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:52.370
And anyways, I walked in,
and I think he was sick.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.960
I mean, he was laying around on a couch.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.327
And he looked up at me, he goes,

00:09:56.327 --> 00:09:58.720
"I don't stand for whores."

00:09:58.720 --> 00:09:59.790
And I go...

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.820
At that point I knew I got the job

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.147
because in "McCabe and Mrs. Miller",

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.613
the only women's parts in
that movie were prostitutes.

00:10:12.935 --> 00:10:17.020
(laughs) And so that's
how I met Robert Altman.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.540
And so from that point
on, I was in that movie.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.500
And when I finished the movie,

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:26.370
I couldn't get any work, you know?

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:31.320
I think I got a job on
a CBC film, a series,

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.960
and I played a receptionist.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:35.260
And so, that was about it.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.530
They weren't casting Asian women.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.330
And this is before
Vancouver became the place

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:47.330
to shoot movies and television shows,

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:51.120
because when I left, they
created an incentive.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.653
So the nickname for
Vancouver is Hollywood North.

00:11:00.268 --> 00:11:02.690
After being unemployed for a while,

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:04.600
I'd take odd jobs just so that,

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.120
you know, pay the rent
and things like that.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.340
And so I finally said to,

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:12.383
once again, my friend Joan in
the basement of her apartment,

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.393
I said, "You know what?

00:11:14.393 --> 00:11:16.350
I've gotta get out of here,"

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.730
because if anybody's
ever been to Vancouver,

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:24.730
they call Vancouver part of
the Northwest Rainforest.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:30.220
So it rains 360 days out of 365 days.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:33.600
And I says, "I gotta get out of here.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.720
I can't stand this rain anymore."

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:36.940
So she says, "Have you thought about

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.760
writing to Robert Altman?"

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.070
And I go, "Well, that's an idea."

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:44.200
So I wrote to him.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:45.600
Never heard back.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.480
And then I read in the trades

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:50.810
that he was doing this picture
called "California Split".

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.950
I had no idea what it was.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:57.950
So I just made a random phone
call to him in California.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:02.840
And I reintroduced myself to him.

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.880
And I said, "You know what?

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.590
I'd like to come to
California, to Los Angeles,

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:09.860
and just sort of shadow you

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.810
because I wanna be a director."

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.480
And he said to me, he
goes, "Do you play poker?"

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.280
And I go, "Well, I can learn.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.240
Sort of.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:21.300
I kinda know how to play poker."

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.300
And he goes, "Okay, be here January 13th."

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.820
I swear to God, within a week I was gone.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:32.300
And I tell people this story because,

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.970
my parents being immigrants,
had very little English...

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.120
Understood very little English.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:44.300
So I said to my mother on
the day I was leaving...

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.393
I mean, they sort of had an
idea I was up to something.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:51.223
And so I left.

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.500
I only knew two people,

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.440
Robert Altman and a kid
I went to church with,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.330
I knew he was going to Art Center.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.300
And I knew I had a place
to stay for a week.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.253
So I had my sleeping bag
and my backpack, and I left.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:09.760
And I'll tell you the
weirdest thing that happened,

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:15.640
was the minute I landed in Los Angeles,

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.110
I knew this was where I belonged.

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:19.943
- That's great.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:22.750
That's fantastic.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.860
- You know, your feet hit
the ground and it was like,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.070
okay, it just...

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.343
This is where you're supposed to be.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.393
- I love that.

00:13:32.393 --> 00:13:33.226
I love that.

00:13:33.226 --> 00:13:34.740
you know, that was just what I would call

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:38.523
a masterclass in moxie, (laughs)

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:43.200
because you just took these small scraps,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.480
or little strings of opportunities

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.200
and turned them into your life.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.560
And it's a fantastic story.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.178
I love it.

00:13:53.178 --> 00:13:55.150
- I think I was dumb to know

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:56.880
that that's not what you do, you know?

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.620
Call up a director, you know, like that.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.580
But yeah.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.160
It was interesting

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.393
because when I got on the set,

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:12.770
I looked around at all these
different departments, right?

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:14.840
And I kinda made a plan in my head.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:16.710
And I go, okay, this is what I'm gonna do.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:20.760
I'm in a situation where
I could learn a lot

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:22.500
from these different people.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:24.290
And so I decided,

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:29.120
okay, I'm gonna create my own
Robert Altman film school.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.560
So every day I would spend...

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.970
'Cause they would see me on the set.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:37.020
The crew would see me on the
set and they always wondered,

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:37.990
who is this person?

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:39.350
What is she doing here?

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:41.920
And so when I explained to them

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:46.920
that I knew Bob and I was
in his movie, and I got...

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.910
It was an interesting dynamic

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:53.070
because then they looked
at me in a different way.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.200
They were very...

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.730
Everybody on the crew was very helpful.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:15:00.400
And I think that I was
protected by knowing him.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.720
So nobody treated me badly,

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.230
even though the guys
were trying to hit on me.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.533
And that was like, (laughs) you know,

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:11.990
part of the environment.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.350
And so I decided, okay, let's see,

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:18.220
today I'm gonna spend the
day with the sound people.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:23.120
And that was with Jimmy
Webb and Chris McLoughlin.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:28.120
And at that time, he was just
creating the 8-track sound.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:33.570
So I would sit with them
and ask them questions.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.200
They were very helpful.

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:39.257
They didn't say, "Beat it, kid."

00:15:40.304 --> 00:15:43.120
And then the next day I would spend it

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:44.810
with the electrical department,

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.000
where I met my future husband.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.280
I didn't know this. (laughs)

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.240
And then the next day I would hang out

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.700
with the camera people,

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:57.700
and the script supervisor
and the wardrobe people

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.540
because I could sew.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:03.610
And I met this lovely wardrobe man.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:06.590
His name was Huey, and he
had been a wardrobe person

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:10.860
for Fred Astaire and Robert Wagner.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:12.430
And he took a liking to me.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:14.050
He was an older gentleman.

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:17.313
And he'd come to work in a
three-piece suit and a fedora.

00:16:18.835 --> 00:16:21.140
So I think he must've been like, 70.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.567
And so he whispered to me, he goes,

00:16:23.567 --> 00:16:25.610
"Okay, this is what you gotta do, kid.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.950
When they call for wardrobe,
or they call 'Huey,'

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:31.380
you go, 'Here.'

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.460
So you replace me.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:36.890
So you run up and you ask
them, 'What do you need?'

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:39.440
Then they'll say, 'Oh,
go to Elliot's trailer

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.050
and grab whatever.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:42.310
And so that's what you do.

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:44.090
So you run and you get the clothes,

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.480
and you come back and
you give it to them."

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:49.665
And so I did that on the set.

00:16:49.665 --> 00:16:51.788
(laughs)

00:16:51.788 --> 00:16:55.470
And of course, as things would happen,

00:16:55.470 --> 00:17:00.230
they went to Reno, Nevada to shoot

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:05.123
some scenes for the casino.

00:17:08.585 --> 00:17:11.310
The script supervisor was pregnant.

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:13.950
And I have to backtrack

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.730
because when I was on the
set of "California Split",

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:20.540
there was one day Alan
Rudolph was the second AD,

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:23.403
assistant director, came
up to me and he goes,

00:17:24.647 --> 00:17:26.500
"Do you know how to hold script?"

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:27.333
I go, "Why?"

00:17:27.333 --> 00:17:29.390
He goes, "Well, Carol's sick.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.500
She's got morning sickness real bad."

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.200
Says, "Well, do you know
how to hold script?"

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.340
I go, "Yeah."

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:37.600
That wasn't true,

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:41.830
because we had video
classes at the college,

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.750
and I knew that you time the stop,

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.640
and then that was about it.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.270
So I go, "Yeah."

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.460
So give me the script.

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:53.230
And here I was, I was holding a script.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:54.390
And I worked for like,

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:59.292
two days as a script
supervisor on this movie.

00:17:59.292 --> 00:18:02.453
And so, anyways.

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:04.870
At that time, Bob looked at me,

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.073
like second day or something.
He look at me and he goes,

00:18:07.073 --> 00:18:08.447
"You know, you actually
do that pretty well."

00:18:08.447 --> 00:18:10.079
I go, "Oh, thanks."

00:18:10.079 --> 00:18:13.710
(laughs) And then from that point on,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:16.100
when we went to Reno,

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:20.010
I also helped with the script supervising.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:24.750
So from that point on, I
just sort of kept hanging out

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:30.550
at his office, 'cause I had
nowhere else to go, you know?

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:34.590
And these were really nice
people, they were fun.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:37.400
- Can you give us a
really brief description

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.120
of what a script supervisor does?

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:40.130
Because a lot of people,

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.210
they don't put those two words together

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:44.220
and have any idea what it means.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.110
Script supervisor.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:50.560
- Well, the script supervisor take notes.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:52.650
She sits next to the...

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:54.650
Well, now they're in video village.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:58.090
But she's the closest
person to the director

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.760
because she has to take
care of the continuity,

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.973
meaning from scene to scene,

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.080
if somebody's wearing a hat in one scene

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:14.080
and the next scene, they
need to be wearing the hat.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:16.350
They can't not be wearing the hat

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.710
because it needs to be consistent.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:23.710
She also takes care of the
script in terms of the timing.

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:31.150
She writes down the lens
that's used in the filming.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:35.500
She makes notes whether
it's a visual effect

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:37.663
that they're shooting, or a plate.

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:40.740
She also hands...

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:42.360
When she's done with the day,

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:46.030
she gives the editors
a log of what was shot

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:50.760
and the script pages of
what was shot that day.

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:55.760
And so that's my guide
to what I'd be seeing

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:57.270
in terms of dailies.

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:01.670
And dailies, that's a word we used

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:06.670
for scenes that was shot the day before.

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:12.150
And so when I get the
dailies the next day,

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.300
that's what it is.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:16.420
Is that clear enough, Dody?
- Yeah, no, that's great.

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:19.880
Yeah, because a lot of
people don't understand

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:23.530
that the script supervisor is
really the connection for us,

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:24.850
especially these days

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:29.010
since we don't watch dailies
together anymore on film.

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:32.100
You're getting a lot of
information from the set

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:35.940
to your editing room through
the script supervisor's notes.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.610
So a lot of times I develop a relationship

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.190
with the script supervisor
for that reason,

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:45.100
so that I can get the information.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.010
And I just think it's interesting.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.220
Also the script supervisor makes sure

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:51.810
that all the words in the script are said

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.511
at least in one take.
- (laughs) Right.

00:20:54.511 --> 00:20:56.140
Right.
- That's-

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.510
- And also the sound report, right?

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.870
You know, sound is very,
very important to us.

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.960
Maybe on the set, they
don't care, you know?

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:10.960
And so the script supervisor's
also my go-between

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:15.290
to talk to the director and to the DP,

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:19.330
the director of photography,
the cinematographer,

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:24.170
because if something goes wrong on the set

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.600
or you have dailies that are confusing,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:31.780
or the camera move is kinda weird

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:34.620
in terms of it being shaky,

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:39.620
or what really bothers
me is when an operator

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:43.990
is shooting a closeup on an actor

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:47.910
and he reframes in the middle of a shot.

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.600
And you go, how am I gonna use that?

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.140
And so instead of going to the producer,

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:54.973
which is a really bad no-no,

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:58.750
I'd go to the script supervisor.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.640
Unless I've been on the set.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:04.100
When I was working with Tyler,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:08.160
I would go to the set the
first week of shooting.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.850
And part of it was to sit with him

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:14.780
because when he first started,
he was a stage director.

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:16.910
And so I would sit with him,

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:19.870
and I'd look at framing,
and I'd, you know,

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:22.680
kinda talk to him a little
bit about the scene.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:24.600
Or I'd just sit and watch.

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:29.430
And I'd develop a relationship
with the cinematographer,

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:32.940
the sound people, the wardrobe
people, the makeup people.

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:37.350
So if anything went wrong after I left,

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:40.957
I can make a phone call to the DP and go,

00:22:40.957 --> 00:22:42.223
"Hey, you know what?

00:22:42.223 --> 00:22:43.860
I just wanna give you a heads up.

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:45.970
Here's what your camera guy's doing."

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.210
I said, "Can you tell him not to do that?"

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.757
So that he then knows that
I'm not threatening him,

00:22:52.757 --> 00:22:55.900
or causing him problems with the producer,

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.220
or even going to the director,

00:22:57.220 --> 00:23:00.350
because the director has
a lot on their minds,

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:02.480
and the last thing they wanna hear is,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.520
okay, camera guy is not doing his job.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:08.743
But on one occasion this happened.

00:23:08.743 --> 00:23:11.600
When I was working with Tyler
on "Daddy's Little Girls"

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:13.980
we had a lot of out of focus shots.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:17.780
And he called me up, he
goes, "What is going on?"

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:22.070
And I go, "Well, I'll
talk to the cameraman

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:24.580
and see what's going on with these guys."

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:27.140
And they said, "Well, the operator said

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:29.410
the actor's moving too
much in this scene."

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.920
I go, "That's their job," you know?

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:35.023
To figure out focus if
they're moving back and forth.

00:23:36.787 --> 00:23:38.500
I said, "That's not a great excuse."

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.920
So from that point on,
Tyler would always ask me,

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:44.210
he goes, "I want you to
tell me what's in focus

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:45.980
and what is out of focus."

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.890
- Well, can I go back a little bit

00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:50.670
and ask you about what
the transition was like

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:53.913
going from acting to post-production
and what prompted it?

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:56.620
You're kind of in the
middle of answering that,

00:23:56.620 --> 00:24:00.510
but then also how your
acting informed your craft

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:03.510
as an editor anyway, in general.

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:07.540
- Well, the transition from
acting to post-production

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:09.300
and eventually to editing

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:13.273
happened when I was
working with Robert Altman.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:20.110
I became a PA on "Nashville",

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:23.803
and I was PA to the wardrobe department.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:29.570
After we finished with "Nashville",

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:31.927
Bob decided that he wanted to do

00:24:31.927 --> 00:24:33.700
"Buffalo Bill and the Indians".

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.820
And it happened that "Buffalo
Bill and the Indians"

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:37.750
was a period piece.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:39.710
It was set in the 1880s,

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:42.920
and so they needed somebody to do research

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.970
on that time period for
the production design,

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:51.420
for the wardrobe, for the dialogue,

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:52.580
and just sort of events

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:55.243
that were happening during the 1880s.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.777
And so he kinda looked at
me and he basically said,

00:24:58.777 --> 00:25:01.920
"Okay, you go do the research."

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:03.170
You know, once again,

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:05.830
sitting in the room when it all happened.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:10.280
And so I started doing the research

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.680
on "Buffalo Bill and the Indians".

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:16.010
And then I went on location with them

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:19.863
and was also the PA to
the wardrobe department.

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:24.580
And after we finished shooting,

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:27.023
when we got back to Los Angeles,

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:34.100
I knew the editors from all
the other movies that Bob did.

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:37.470
And so one day I'm
walking down the hallway,

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:41.583
and back in the day of
film, we had trimmed-ins,

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:45.720
where you put the film,
you put the film on hooks,

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:50.560
you'd mark it, and it all
had to go back into rolls

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:55.560
so that the editor, when
they put it up on the KEM,

00:25:56.120 --> 00:26:00.330
or the Steenbeck, that
everything was in sync.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:04.350
And so there must've
been 20 of these bins.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:09.350
And I'm walking down the hall
and I see two pair of legs,

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:14.770
it was like a cartoon, inside this bin.

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:18.000
And you see all this film flying out,

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:20.280
which was really another big no-no

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:25.280
because you can't have
any scratches on film.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:28.410
And as an assistant if you tear film,

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.030
then you'd have to get a reprint,

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:34.660
which meant it would cost
the company more money.

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:37.470
So I tap this guy.

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:39.123
And I knew who it was.

00:26:39.123 --> 00:26:40.987
And I go, "What are you doing?"

00:26:40.987 --> 00:26:44.047
And he goes, "I'm looking for two frames."

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:49.770
Now, we're on electronic
editing equipment now.

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:51.560
Two frames is nothing, you know?

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:55.900
But back in the day when
you've got 20 bins of film

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:58.050
and you gotta look for the two frames,

00:26:58.050 --> 00:26:59.610
you need somebody to help you.

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:00.983
So I looked at him, I go,

00:27:01.857 --> 00:27:03.700
"Well, it looks like you need help."

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.030
He goes, "Yes.

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:06.250
We need an apprentice.

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:09.050
We need somebody to put
the stuff together."

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:10.730
And I go, "Well, I think I can do it."

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:13.300
He goes, "Yeah, you can
most definitely do it."

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:14.940
I said, "So?"

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:16.940
He goes, "Well, you gotta go ask Bob."

00:27:16.940 --> 00:27:19.010
I go, "Oh, god."

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:23.640
Okay, so I walked across the way,

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:27.170
and at that time, Robert Altman, to me,

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.580
was like seven feet tall.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:32.070
In reality, he was probably 6'1", okay?

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:33.560
I'm five-feet-one.

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.270
And so I walk in and he's pacing.

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:39.970
And I was trying to test
what mood he was in.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:42.380
And so anyways, I go, okay fine,

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:43.770
what is he gonna say, no?

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:46.763
So I walk up, open the
door and walked in, I go,

00:27:48.057 --> 00:27:52.430
"Bob, you know, the editors need help

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:55.960
in reconstituting the film."

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:58.410
And I says, "I think I can do it.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.397
They need an apprentice."

00:27:59.397 --> 00:28:01.207
And he looked down at me, he goes,

00:28:01.207 --> 00:28:03.120
"This isn't a film school, you know?"

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:08.120
And I go, "Well, I know
that, but you know what?

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:09.580
They still need help."

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:11.907
And he kinda looked at me, he goes,

00:28:11.907 --> 00:28:14.280
"Okay, if it's okay with the
guys, it's okay with me."

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:15.680
And I go, "It's okay with the guys."

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:18.170
And man, I ran out of that place so fast

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.040
before he changed his mind.

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:23.610
So that's how I got into editing.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:28.080
I became an assistant on "Buffalo Bill"

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:31.040
because he shot like,
a million feet of film,

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.040
so it had to be organized.

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:35.650
They had three editors on the show.

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:39.190
And so that's basically
how I got into editing.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:44.190
And from that, I got a huge
lesson in post-production,

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:50.460
how a film is shot.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:54.210
And in my background,
I've been so fortunate

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:55.660
growing up in this business,

00:28:55.660 --> 00:29:00.660
seeing amazing dailies, knowing where...

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:02.830
Working with a director that knows

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:05.300
exactly where to put the camera.

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:09.720
So when I'm working now,
you just get this feeling.

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:14.720
It's ingrained in my bones that
I know when it's a good shot

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:19.340
or when it just like, oh,
that just doesn't feel right,

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:24.340
because I sat in the room
when Bob would come in

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:27.393
and give notes to the editors.

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:32.710
And I'd sit there and I'd
listen to what he had to say.

00:29:32.710 --> 00:29:35.940
And I would kinda look at the film

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:40.930
and kinda try to maybe second guess,

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:42.940
or guess what he was gonna say.

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:47.550
And so that's how I got
into post-production.

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:50.280
And the reason why I decided

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:55.280
that I wanted to be in
editing was because acting,

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:01.210
I didn't want to go out for the one line,

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:06.210
the one line when I was 30,
40, 50, 60, 70, you know?

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.920
And I said, "You know,
if I became an editor,

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:13.580
they couldn't judge me
on the way I looked.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:18.390
They would judge me on my
resume, my body of work.

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:21.410
And if they did, then well
it's too bad for them."

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:22.243
You know?

00:30:22.243 --> 00:30:25.340
I don't particularly wanna
work with people like that.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.350
And so in editing a film,

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:31.130
I think that the acting helped me

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:36.130
because I would find the
truth of the character.

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:38.120
You know, when you're acting

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:40.490
you've gotta find the motivation,

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:43.020
you gotta figure out their backstory.

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:44.960
And so as an editor,

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:48.530
I try to put myself in as a character.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:49.890
- Yeah, that's right.

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:52.150
All the little moments
you're always looking for.

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:55.690
But that's a great lead-in
to this other question

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:59.033
of how you find the best
moments in and out of a scene?

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:03.313
- Well, I think you sit and you watch,

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:08.203
I guess with an actor's
eye and an editor's eye.

00:31:09.734 --> 00:31:14.480
And you just listen to the performance

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:18.140
and you watch the
behavior of the character.

00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:21.420
And this is where, leaving the scene,

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:22.630
I know about timing

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:26.450
because that's where my
improv background comes in.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:28.670
Because when you're on the stage

00:31:28.670 --> 00:31:30.390
and you're just sort of winging it,

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:31.840
and you have this feeling like,

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:35.060
oh my god, I think I'm
dying in this scene.

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:37.400
Then somebody comes in and saves you.

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:41.490
And so having learned that

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:44.860
and having participated in improvs,

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:46.840
you just get a feeling.

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:48.820
So I think that as editors,

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:52.107
there's a lot of times
when a director will say,

00:31:52.107 --> 00:31:53.730
"Why did you pick that take?"

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:57.110
And I go, "Well, 'cause it felt right."

00:31:57.110 --> 00:31:57.943
- Absolutely.

00:31:57.943 --> 00:31:59.827
- Basically, you're not gonna say,

00:31:59.827 --> 00:32:02.310
"Well, I'm discounting your feelings,"

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:05.730
because that's a whole
other (laughs) conversation.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:09.610
But you just feel the scene.

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:14.050
And for any of the students
wanting to get into editing,

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:16.520
you have to trust your
instincts, you know?

00:32:16.520 --> 00:32:21.000
- That's another great
lead-in to this question of,

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:23.470
if a director is especially attached

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:25.410
to one shot or sequence

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:28.700
and you believe it kills the
momentum of the film at large,

00:32:28.700 --> 00:32:31.270
how do you have that conversation?

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:33.640
You were touching on it right there.

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:35.023
Trusting your instincts.

00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:38.590
- Well, you have a very
gingerly conversation

00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:41.160
because their egos are about, you know?

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:42.283
Or about this big.

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:46.430
What I do, is I propose it.

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:47.533
I'll say, "You know what?

00:32:47.533 --> 00:32:50.490
I don't think we need this shot,"

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:54.290
or "I feel it's slowing the momentum."

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:55.880
And they go like, "No, I don't think so."

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.290
And you go, "Okay," you leave it alone.

00:32:58.290 --> 00:32:59.123
Work on the other scenes.

00:32:59.123 --> 00:33:01.827
Then you come back and then you go,

00:33:01.827 --> 00:33:02.660
"You know what?

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:07.660
I still feel that it's
stopping the movie."

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:09.587
And they'll kinda look at me and I go,

00:33:09.587 --> 00:33:11.820
"Okay, I'm dropping it.

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:15.860
I'm not gonna revisit
this until the very end."

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:18.470
And so as we're being brutal

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:21.530
with putting our movie in shape,

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:24.513
that's when we become ruthless.

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:29.010
Ruthless in the way
you're servicing the film,

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:32.320
not ruthless because he
editor has an ego that,

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:36.170
oh, you know, okay, two
points for him and two for me

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:37.970
so now we're at a draw.

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:39.770
And so I bring it back up.

00:33:39.770 --> 00:33:42.400
And I go, "You know, I've been watching it

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:45.977
and the last three cuts," I said,

00:33:45.977 --> 00:33:48.602
"It's stopping the scene

00:33:48.602 --> 00:33:51.790
and stopping the momentum of the movie."

00:33:51.790 --> 00:33:56.040
And they'll finally go, "Yeah, yeah.

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:57.960
All right, take it out."

00:33:57.960 --> 00:34:00.170
Or if there is a scene

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:04.570
that is really in between
two other scenes, right?

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:07.780
And you go, "We don't need this scene.

00:34:07.780 --> 00:34:10.970
We already know what's gonna happen.

00:34:10.970 --> 00:34:15.110
The dialogue is giving us
foreshadowing everything,

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:16.670
so we don't need it."

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:18.487
And they'll kinda look at me and I go,

00:34:18.487 --> 00:34:20.130
"Here's what you do.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:21.900
Put it in your next movie.

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:24.707
Get that little card and
put it in your next movie."

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:27.850
And I think I do it with humor

00:34:27.850 --> 00:34:32.850
because our job is to
facilitate their vision,

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:38.620
and the ego is creating the
best film we can together,

00:34:40.220 --> 00:34:42.610
and being a good collaborator.

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:45.010
And then there are, maybe once in a while,

00:34:45.010 --> 00:34:48.450
there are editors, or no, directors,

00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:49.693
that are writers.

00:34:51.802 --> 00:34:55.740
When I'm interviewing with a
director who's also a writer,

00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:58.750
and my question is, "Are
you going to be holding on

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:00.987
to things that you've written?"

00:35:00.987 --> 00:35:03.710
"Oh, no, no, no, no, I'm
really good with that."

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:05.317
And then when you get down it it's like,

00:35:05.317 --> 00:35:08.890
"No, we really need that scene."

00:35:08.890 --> 00:35:10.240
And I'd look at them, I go,

00:35:12.017 --> 00:35:14.737
"What did you tell me in
that first interview?"

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:21.480
And so it is sitting and
watching the movie a lot.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:24.237
And that's when people ask, they say,

00:35:24.237 --> 00:35:25.470
"Well, don't you get bored

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:27.657
watching the scene over and over?"

00:35:27.657 --> 00:35:28.940
And I go, "Well no,

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.067
because I'm looking at different things."

00:35:32.139 --> 00:35:33.310
I don't know about you, Dody,

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:38.090
but we're always looking
at the whole frame.

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:41.756
It isn't just the performance.

00:35:41.756 --> 00:35:42.589
- Absolutely.

00:35:42.589 --> 00:35:47.160
And what I'm hearing is this
really intimate relationship

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:49.800
that you're developing
with your directors.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.550
So you have those
conversations in the interview,

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.530
and then you actually are bringing it back

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:56.880
when you're working together.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:00.413
And I think that's fantastic.

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:04.290
So I have a few more questions.

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:07.290
These come from some of
the students in Kenya.

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:12.290
If you have any interaction
with them on the color grading

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:15.560
with the director in
the finishing product,

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:17.670
and how that works,

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.488
and if you wanna say anything about that.

00:36:20.488 --> 00:36:23.553
And how it affects the
overall emotion of the film.

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:29.530
- Well, I think that our job,

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:34.090
we start our job when
they first start shooting.

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:36.460
Though I wish that we started sooner

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:41.740
because I think we have a
lot of input into scenes

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:46.463
and the overall look, or
the sound that's involved.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.960
So I'll give you sort of a timeline

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:52.393
and that'll explain that question.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:58.290
Editors start on the
first day of shooting,

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:01.220
and then they finish shooting,

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:04.000
we present our cut to the director,

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:06.140
the director looks at it,

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:09.370
and then at that time we
start having discussions

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:12.890
about the sound of a scene,

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:15.223
the overall look of the movie.

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:19.610
And so once he's happy with his cut

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:22.240
and we're locking the picture,

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:27.240
I stay on to supervise
the color correction,

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:32.870
the sound of the movie, we're on the mix,

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:36.973
the movie, we talk to the composer.

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:43.040
And we're the keeper of
how it looks, you know?

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:46.290
We speak for the director.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.490
I mean, when I'm in a situation,

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:50.850
when I'm working with Tyler,

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:55.220
he leaves after his cut and I take over.

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:58.230
And I do all of the color correction,

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:01.610
I'm on the stage listening to the sound,

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:04.310
making sure it's what he wants,

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:06.873
and talking to the composer.

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:13.000
The music, where it should be cued.

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:17.010
We have sessions, what we
call spotting sessions,

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:22.010
meaning the sound effects
supervisor comes into my room

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:26.860
along with the visual
effects supervisor, you know?

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:30.770
And the music supervisor and the composer.

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:33.340
So we sit and we run through the movie,

00:38:33.340 --> 00:38:34.830
and we stop and go.

00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:38.403
And the composer would
say, or I would say,

00:38:39.827 --> 00:38:41.600
"Tyler wants music in here,"

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:44.750
or they just get a feel of it.

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:47.853
And we're talking through
this whole process.

00:38:49.286 --> 00:38:54.190
And the sound supervisor
gets to see the whole movie,

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:59.010
so he has an idea of
what it should sound like

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:00.333
in certain scenes.

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:04.100
And then we go off and we do our jobs,

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:09.100
and the composer invites
me to listen to the score.

00:39:10.990 --> 00:39:13.440
And I tell people, "Look, you know what?

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:15.710
I'm here representing Tyler,"

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:17.080
and I'm here representing

00:39:17.080 --> 00:39:18.870
any of the directors I've worked with.

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:23.460
I said, "So, if I ask you to do something

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:28.150
and you think it's not
something you wanna do,"

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:30.330
I said, "Well, you know,
first of all let me know

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:31.640
and we talk about it.

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:34.910
But a bottom line is,
if it gets screwed up

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:36.787
then I'll take the responsibility.

00:39:36.787 --> 00:39:39.590
I'm not gonna put it on you.

00:39:39.590 --> 00:39:42.847
But we're here to do a job together."

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:49.830
Which leads me, in terms of the sound

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:52.380
and the color of the picture,

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:55.180
when we were doing "The Joy Luck Club",

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:58.570
if you examine that, if you
look at that movie closely,

00:39:58.570 --> 00:40:03.170
there are no dissolves or
fade-outs in that entire movie,

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:05.910
in that two hour and 15 minute movie.

00:40:05.910 --> 00:40:07.623
And the reason for that was,

00:40:08.460 --> 00:40:11.910
when I was talking to
Wayne Weng, the director,

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:15.490
I said, "How are we gonna
deal with transitions?"

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:19.430
I said, "I'm not a fan of
dissolves or fade-outs,

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:22.280
but if you want them, we're gonna do it."

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:24.950
And he goes, "Well, we're
gonna do an A-B on it,"

00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:28.360
which means they do it in the lab.

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:33.360
You don't get to see it
before it goes to the lab.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:36.810
And I said to him, I go,
"Well, that's scary."

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:37.900
I said, "I don't like that,

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:42.900
because I don't get to see
how the dissolve works."

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:45.390
Because sometimes you put
a dissolve in and you go,

00:40:45.390 --> 00:40:48.100
oh my god, you gotta
start noodling with it.

00:40:48.100 --> 00:40:49.890
But because they do it in the lab,

00:40:49.890 --> 00:40:51.710
it's done.

00:40:51.710 --> 00:40:55.600
You can't undo it unless you
go back and redo that thing.

00:40:55.600 --> 00:40:58.363
So I said to Wayne, he and I decided,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:00.410
well, how about this?

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:03.030
Why don't we do sound effects?

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:08.030
So in that movie, the four
women were different elements.

00:41:10.210 --> 00:41:15.000
One was water, one was fire,
one was wood, one was wind.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:20.000
And so especially the scene where Lindo,

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:24.480
the woman that gets married
off to this young boy,

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.883
when she decides to get out
of this arranged marriage.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:31.810
At the end when she

00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:36.700
tells her mother-in-law

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:41.700
that the maid is actually
carrying her son's baby,

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:45.880
and that's how she got
out of that marriage.

00:41:45.880 --> 00:41:50.220
And so the line goes, "Well,
Huang Taitai got her grandson

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:52.660
and I got a one-way ticket to Shanghai."

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:56.230
And so in that scene, when
you hear that narration,

00:41:56.230 --> 00:42:01.230
the narration bridges us
into the past-present.

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:06.950
And on top of that, you
hear the sound of a train

00:42:06.950 --> 00:42:11.950
crossing that scene, and
it morphs into cable car,

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:15.990
which is, now we're in San Francisco.

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:16.970
- That's great.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:17.970
I love that.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:18.803
I love that.

00:42:18.803 --> 00:42:22.360
So you're using audio as your
transition instead of visual.

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:26.590
And if it's effective
and it's doing all that,

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:30.000
then it brings the audience in.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.030
Or like I like to say,

00:42:32.030 --> 00:42:34.920
the sound sucks you into the next scene.

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:35.753
- Right.

00:42:35.753 --> 00:42:37.580
And we did it throughout
the whole movie, you know?

00:42:37.580 --> 00:42:39.910
On a subliminal level.

00:42:39.910 --> 00:42:44.910
And it worked really well in that movie.

00:42:48.790 --> 00:42:52.910
Which brings me to, I think
there was a question about,

00:42:52.910 --> 00:42:56.950
are there things that we create
that are not in the script

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:00.823
to help forward the movie?

00:43:01.970 --> 00:43:04.827
And a scene, once again
"The Joy Luck Club",

00:43:05.890 --> 00:43:10.890
where the scene is with the same woman,

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:16.370
Ying-Ying and her daughter
living in this crooked house.

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:18.180
And so she's walking around,

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:20.980
and her whole thing is, "I'm
like a tiger on the prowl."

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:24.070
So Wayne comes into my cutting room

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:25.620
and he had this look on his face.

00:43:25.620 --> 00:43:26.710
I go, "What's going on?"

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:29.080
And he goes, "Well, I've
been accused of this,

00:43:29.080 --> 00:43:31.770
and I guess I'll be accused of it again."

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:33.010
I go, "What is that?"

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:35.200
He goes, "Being abstract."

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:36.370
I go, "Well, let's talk about it."

00:43:36.370 --> 00:43:37.800
I go, "What's on your mind?"

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:42.800
And he goes, "Well, I'm
thinking I'm not quite sure

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:48.320
how we bridge this scene
where she's walking around,

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:52.707
and it feels like it could be too long."

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:58.447
And I said, "Well, what
do you think about this?"

00:43:59.310 --> 00:44:01.660
'Cause editors and directors,

00:44:01.660 --> 00:44:04.070
we actually spend a lot of time talking.

00:44:04.070 --> 00:44:06.777
A lot people, when they walk
by the cutting room they go,

00:44:06.777 --> 00:44:08.800
"Oh, they're just talking,
they're not working."

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:10.890
But we get a lot of work done

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:13.470
just talking and delving into things.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:15.227
And I said to him, I go,

00:44:15.227 --> 00:44:19.770
"Here's a woman that
kills her baby, you know?

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:21.730
It's 20 years later."

00:44:21.730 --> 00:44:23.450
I said, "She's still gonna be

00:44:23.450 --> 00:44:26.910
thinking about that almost daily."

00:44:26.910 --> 00:44:31.170
I said, "What if we just
cut a bunch of stuff

00:44:31.170 --> 00:44:34.680
that builds up to that moment?

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:39.680
And then he had shot the vase breaking,

00:44:40.959 --> 00:44:45.477
and it kinda cues the woman
and her husband to look up.

00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:51.520
The noise cues them,
something's happened upstairs.

00:44:51.520 --> 00:44:55.130
And so that's how that montage came out,

00:44:55.130 --> 00:44:56.930
that you see the baby,

00:44:56.930 --> 00:45:01.800
you see the bad husband
and the watermelon,

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:05.340
and we accentuated all
those sounds, you know?

00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:09.790
And so at the end is when the vase breaks,

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:13.050
and once again, it
echoes through the house,

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:16.410
and that cues the daughter to come up

00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:20.113
and to get the final lesson.

00:45:21.204 --> 00:45:22.340
- That's great.

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:23.226
I love that.

00:45:23.226 --> 00:45:25.240
- That wasn't in the script at all.

00:45:25.240 --> 00:45:30.240
So that was a fun one to
work on, create that montage.

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:36.670
- That's true invention
with the material you have,

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:39.650
and really going above and beyond.

00:45:39.650 --> 00:45:41.300
And from what you're describing,

00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:45.850
obviously your career has
been so busy and jam-packed.

00:45:45.850 --> 00:45:49.230
How did you balance work and family,

00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:50.480
and coming back to work

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:52.830
after some time off with your children?

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:54.770
How did you balance all that?

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:56.813
And how did you manage to come back?

00:45:58.450 --> 00:46:03.260
- Well, I decided to take a hiatus from

00:46:03.260 --> 00:46:07.900
working as a sound editor, actually,

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:11.190
because when I worked with Bob,

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:13.243
like on "3 Women", for instance,

00:46:15.080 --> 00:46:16.913
he put me in that movie.

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:20.670
And how I got that job was, once again,

00:46:20.670 --> 00:46:22.837
I'm walking down the hall
and he looks at me, he goes,

00:46:22.837 --> 00:46:24.910
"You're Doris and you're
going to Palm Springs."

00:46:24.910 --> 00:46:26.650
I go, "Okay."

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:28.143
I don't know what that means.

00:46:30.200 --> 00:46:33.280
But the backstory to that,
there's always a backstory here,

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:36.170
is that I was living with Scotty Bushnell.

00:46:36.170 --> 00:46:37.620
We were sharing an apartment.

00:46:37.620 --> 00:46:40.220
And one morning the phone rings at like,

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:41.310
6:00 in the morning,

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:45.640
and she's on the phone
for at least two hours.

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:48.030
I get up, shower, I go sit down,

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:49.290
have coffee with her.

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:51.703
And she goes, "Well, that was Bob."

00:46:51.703 --> 00:46:52.853
I go, "Oh, really?

00:46:53.811 --> 00:46:54.644
What did he want?"

00:46:54.644 --> 00:46:57.410
And he goes, "Well, he
had a dream (laughs)

00:46:57.410 --> 00:47:01.330
we're doing a movie called
'3 Women'." (laughs)

00:47:02.350 --> 00:47:06.540
So that was the reference-
(indistinct)

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:10.940
Which is crazy, right?
- Love it.

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:13.627
- So that's when he pointed to me like,

00:47:13.627 --> 00:47:15.010
"Okay, you're Doris,"

00:47:15.010 --> 00:47:17.750
who was one of the spa workers.

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:20.440
It turned out Doris was
one of the spa workers.

00:47:20.440 --> 00:47:24.830
And so I went to Palm Springs.

00:47:24.830 --> 00:47:27.220
And I went to Palm Springs
not only as an actress

00:47:27.220 --> 00:47:28.993
but an assistant film editor.

00:47:29.890 --> 00:47:32.770
So I was doing double duty here.

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:37.770
And when I was finished acting,

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:43.890
I would help Dennis, my editor,
put the dailies together.

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:48.853
And so, from that point on,

00:47:48.853 --> 00:47:51.280
then I would work in...

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:57.830
When we got back, I transitioned
to working in sound.

00:47:58.070 --> 00:48:01.400
And so then I became a sound assistant.

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:06.400
And so, one day I'm working on the show,

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:11.620
and this great guy, another mentor,

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:14.480
Bill Stevenson said to me, he
goes, "What are you doing?"

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.910
He goes, "You know,
you're a sound assistant.

00:48:16.910 --> 00:48:20.283
You're never gonna get
a job being an editor.

00:48:22.138 --> 00:48:25.400
You need to go pursue that."

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:26.960
And I kinda looked at
him, I go, "You know what?

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:27.793
You're right."

00:48:27.793 --> 00:48:29.250
And so I said, "You know what?

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:30.690
I gotta take time off."

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:35.120
I said, "I gotta regroup
and figure this out."

00:48:35.120 --> 00:48:39.800
And so that figuring out took eight years.

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:41.190
So I was on hiatus.

00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:42.260
I didn't have a job.

00:48:42.260 --> 00:48:45.480
I decided I was having a family,

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:49.870
and it just turned into
an eight-year hold.

00:48:52.430 --> 00:48:54.320
My husband's also in the business.

00:48:54.320 --> 00:48:56.053
He was a gaffer.

00:48:57.490 --> 00:48:59.700
So he worked, and I stayed at home

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:01.503
taking care of my two boys.

00:49:02.510 --> 00:49:06.270
But I did something, I
guess it was intuitively.

00:49:06.270 --> 00:49:09.590
And this is advice I give to men and women

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:12.050
who are deciding to start a family.

00:49:12.050 --> 00:49:16.500
I said, "Every month I would
call up Bill Stevenson,

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:19.420
or I'd call up the editors
that I had work with,

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:22.530
and I go 'Hey, you guys wanna
go have coffee or lunch?'"

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:26.060
And we'd spend a couple hours
having coffee and lunch.

00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:31.060
And so then my husband
had a really bad year.

00:49:33.290 --> 00:49:35.440
He had worked with this one cameraman

00:49:35.440 --> 00:49:38.740
and they had a big falling out,

00:49:38.740 --> 00:49:40.730
so my husband was out of work,

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:43.610
and I was out of work for a whole year.

00:49:43.610 --> 00:49:45.097
And so I said to him, I go,

00:49:45.097 --> 00:49:48.710
"Okay, I gotta get back to work,"

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:52.940
because I'm a member
of the Editor's Guild,

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:54.360
which is a union.

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.780
And I was in good standing
'cause I paid my dues.

00:49:57.780 --> 00:50:00.447
And so I called up Bill, and I said,

00:50:00.447 --> 00:50:02.557
"Hey, Bill Stevenson," I said,

00:50:02.557 --> 00:50:04.320
"I'm looking for a job.

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:05.637
Do you have a job?"

00:50:05.637 --> 00:50:07.327
And he said to me, he goes,

00:50:07.327 --> 00:50:11.417
"Well, I don't have anything
for at least a month."

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:14.940
He goes, "But I can't pay you much,"

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:16.800
'cause he was always cheap, right?

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:19.470
And he goes, "I could only
pay you as an apprentice."

00:50:19.470 --> 00:50:20.760
I go, "I don't care."

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:23.140
At that time, I think an
apprentice was making like,

00:50:23.140 --> 00:50:28.140
$700 a week, which is better
than answering phones.

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:31.860
And so that's what happened.

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:34.030
I worked with him for like...

00:50:35.412 --> 00:50:39.260
When his show came in,
he honored that promised,

00:50:39.260 --> 00:50:41.010
and I worked with him.

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:46.010
And then during that
time, I was working...

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:50.630
I went to a wedding,
Raja Gosnell's wedding,

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:54.890
who used to be our, Dennis and I,

00:50:54.890 --> 00:50:58.030
he was our apprentice and assistant.

00:50:58.030 --> 00:51:00.450
Well, now he's a big-time director.

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:02.110
And he was getting married,

00:51:02.110 --> 00:51:07.110
and so I saw Dennis at the reception.

00:51:07.220 --> 00:51:09.880
And where else would you
meet an editor but at a bar?

00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:11.590
And so we stood there,

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:14.230
and he goes, "Hey, I hear you're getting

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:15.410
back in the business."

00:51:15.410 --> 00:51:16.320
I go, "Yeah."

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:17.153
I said, "Why?

00:51:17.153 --> 00:51:18.113
You have a job for me"

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:20.380
We had a really good relationship

00:51:20.380 --> 00:51:22.180
that we could bicker like that, right?

00:51:22.180 --> 00:51:23.987
And I go, "What?

00:51:23.987 --> 00:51:25.147
You looking for an assistant?"

00:51:25.147 --> 00:51:28.150
And he goes, "Yeah, as a matter of fact."

00:51:28.150 --> 00:51:30.120
I go, "Oh, really?"

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:32.070
I go, "What happened to
your other assistant?"

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:34.200
I said, "Did she find a better editor?"

00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:37.220
And so he goes, "Yeah, she
went on to greener pastures.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:38.410
Anyways, do you want a job?"

00:51:38.410 --> 00:51:39.410
I go, "Yeah.

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:40.740
Yeah, I need a job."

00:51:40.740 --> 00:51:43.217
And so he hired me to do "Major League".

00:51:44.270 --> 00:51:48.950
So that's how I got back as an assistant.

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:53.937
Then I met Danny Greene
because after that show,

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:56.680
Dennis had another job.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:59.800
And so there was a
post-production supervisor,

00:51:59.800 --> 00:52:03.800
Gloria Clark, and she was really nice.

00:52:03.800 --> 00:52:06.350
So that job fell through,

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:08.650
but I helped her on another job.

00:52:08.650 --> 00:52:11.463
I went over to her house and
helped her with the list.

00:52:11.463 --> 00:52:14.020
I mean, I was just doing my job.

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:17.060
And so what happened was,

00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:19.160
she had a friend named Danny Greene

00:52:19.160 --> 00:52:22.860
who was a very well-known editor.

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:25.097
He cut "Blazing Saddles", "M*A*S*H",

00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:28.600
very well-established editor.

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:30.610
And she said, "Oh, I have a friend

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:31.890
who's looking for an assistant."

00:52:31.890 --> 00:52:33.110
I go, "Really?"

00:52:33.110 --> 00:52:35.580
And she said, "Yeah.

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:37.500
Can I give him your name?"

00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:39.490
I said, "Sure."

00:52:39.490 --> 00:52:43.690
And he calls me, and
interviews me over the phone.

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:45.653
And I get hired by Danny Greene.

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:49.100
So I go off and I work with Danny.

00:52:49.100 --> 00:52:54.100
And God rest his soul,
Danny's passed away,

00:52:54.100 --> 00:52:56.940
but he became a great mentor to me.

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:58.842
And he was super old school,

00:52:58.842 --> 00:53:03.610
because back in the day,
editor's loved to go have lunch

00:53:04.670 --> 00:53:07.640
in a place where they had a bar. (laughs)

00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:09.123
Right?
- Yep.

00:53:10.127 --> 00:53:11.490
- And so I-

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:14.493
- (indistinct) Soup to go.

00:53:14.493 --> 00:53:16.093
- (laughs) Right?

00:53:17.180 --> 00:53:18.390
Wink wink, nudge nudge,

00:53:18.390 --> 00:53:21.840
let's go have a lunch at a shi-shi place.

00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:23.270
That's what he would call it.

00:53:23.270 --> 00:53:26.260
And so we'd go to Musso and Frank's.

00:53:26.260 --> 00:53:28.253
Oh my god, we'd go all over Hollywood.

00:53:29.410 --> 00:53:31.733
But I noticed that when we'd come back,

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:35.140
he'd sort of sit down at the couch,

00:53:35.140 --> 00:53:36.430
and then he'd have the trades,

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:39.293
and the next thing you
know, he was asleep.

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:45.400
So this happened like, the whole week.

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:47.360
And before the end of the week,

00:53:47.360 --> 00:53:49.253
Wednesday or something like that,

00:53:49.253 --> 00:53:51.970
I go, great, so what am I gonna do?

00:53:51.970 --> 00:53:53.960
Read the script again?

00:53:53.960 --> 00:53:57.420
So finally one day,

00:53:57.420 --> 00:53:59.530
I knew he was just
about ready to doze off.

00:53:59.530 --> 00:54:00.717
I go, "Listen," I go,

00:54:00.717 --> 00:54:04.027
"Hey, Danny, would you
mind if I work on a scene?"

00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:07.630
And he goes, "Knock
yourself out, darlin',"

00:54:07.630 --> 00:54:09.413
and off, (snores) off he'd go.

00:54:10.941 --> 00:54:14.313
(laughs) So, I'd work on a scene.

00:54:15.661 --> 00:54:17.530
And the only reason I asked him that

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:21.380
was because we were working in tandem.

00:54:21.380 --> 00:54:23.247
So he'd be working on the bigger machine,

00:54:23.247 --> 00:54:25.390
I'd work on the smaller machine,

00:54:25.390 --> 00:54:27.050
and he'd go, "Okay, at this point

00:54:27.050 --> 00:54:29.650
I need a closeup on this actor, okay?"

00:54:29.650 --> 00:54:32.590
Off I go, I look, cut it, hand it to him.

00:54:32.590 --> 00:54:34.620
He'd splice it in.

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:36.360
This would go on.

00:54:36.360 --> 00:54:39.370
He never said to me, he
goes, "Why'd you pick that?"

00:54:39.370 --> 00:54:41.260
He'd just put it in and then we'd play it.

00:54:41.260 --> 00:54:44.643
So I kinda knew that we were in sync.

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:47.197
So I finally said to him, I go,

00:54:47.197 --> 00:54:48.910
"Okay, hey listen, Danny,

00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:53.480
while you're relaxing, would
you mind if I work on a scene?"

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:55.820
And he goes, "Oh, knock
yourself out, darlin'."

00:54:55.820 --> 00:54:59.090
- Relaxing.
- Relaxing.

00:54:59.090 --> 00:55:00.470
Anyways.

00:55:00.470 --> 00:55:02.110
So I'd work on a scene.

00:55:02.110 --> 00:55:05.177
Then about 5:00 I'd go, tap, tap, tap,

00:55:05.177 --> 00:55:08.560
"Hey, Danny, before we go home,

00:55:08.560 --> 00:55:11.400
would you mind looking
at what I did today?"

00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:14.143
And he'd get up, he'd look at the scene.

00:55:15.010 --> 00:55:19.880
And he'd look at it, and
he'd take something out

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:24.730
and then splice it together
or rearrange something.

00:55:24.730 --> 00:55:26.540
And then he goes, "There."

00:55:26.540 --> 00:55:27.650
I'd go, "Oh, great.

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:28.483
Thank you."

00:55:28.483 --> 00:55:31.130
So then the next day
I'd do the same thing.

00:55:31.130 --> 00:55:32.787
And he'd look at the scene, and he goes,

00:55:32.787 --> 00:55:33.920
"What's wrong with it?"

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:37.060
I go, "I don't know.

00:55:37.060 --> 00:55:37.893
It feels good."

00:55:37.893 --> 00:55:40.110
And he goes, "Yeah, it's great."

00:55:40.110 --> 00:55:42.920
So that was our...

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:47.920
That was my mentorship that I
actually had a teacher there.

00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:49.850
And not one time did he look at me like,

00:55:49.850 --> 00:55:52.790
well, that's a stupid cut, or
what are you thinking about?

00:55:52.790 --> 00:55:57.790
And so he became very
helpful in my transition.

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:03.710
- That's great because he's
giving you license to edit.

00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:06.780
You didn't have to get
the job to do the editing.

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:11.580
You were there and he was
mentoring you in the moment,

00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:14.920
right on site there,
and it wasn't a question

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:17.150
about someone having to believe in you

00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:19.140
when you didn't have an editing credit.

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:21.600
I have one more question.

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:26.600
Do you have any advice
for student film editors?

00:56:28.850 --> 00:56:31.710
- Well, I think the important thing is,

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:36.710
if you're going to school
and there's a film program,

00:56:36.780 --> 00:56:41.780
volunteer to cut the master
thesis for the students.

00:56:43.760 --> 00:56:48.760
Any kind of hands-on will
help you in the future.

00:56:51.190 --> 00:56:54.290
And don't be afraid to tell people

00:56:54.290 --> 00:56:56.410
what your goal is, you know?

00:56:56.410 --> 00:56:58.190
What you wanna do,

00:56:58.190 --> 00:57:00.240
because there are a lot of people,

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:02.000
as you can tell, there are a lot of people

00:57:02.000 --> 00:57:03.660
that are willing to help you

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:08.520
if you stay enthusiastic,
you keep out of people's way.

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:11.790
And the main thing is to
listen and to observe,

00:57:11.790 --> 00:57:14.800
because by listening and observing

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:16.650
the way Robert Altman worked,

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:20.780
I was able to formulate
my own style of editing.

00:57:20.780 --> 00:57:25.150
And I think that there's
so many opportunities now

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:28.293
for people to do what we do.

00:57:30.018 --> 00:57:32.050
And if you can't go to a school,

00:57:32.050 --> 00:57:34.843
just lookin the want ads.

00:57:36.100 --> 00:57:38.080
Because I'm sure that in the want ads

00:57:38.080 --> 00:57:43.080
there're industrials, films
are really in demand too,

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:49.130
because you know that Microsoft,

00:57:49.130 --> 00:57:52.040
and my son who is an editor,

00:57:52.040 --> 00:57:55.030
he did a lot of promos for Microsoft.

00:57:55.030 --> 00:57:59.000
Now, you never would think that
Microsoft or Google or Yahoo

00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:01.483
would have that kind of department.

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:07.410
Or any major, like Proctor and Gamble.

00:58:07.510 --> 00:58:10.840
I mean, those places, I think...

00:58:10.840 --> 00:58:11.673
And even in then...

00:58:11.673 --> 00:58:15.628
Well, I don't wanna suggest
anybody join the Army,

00:58:15.628 --> 00:58:20.628
(laughs) but those areas have film editing

00:58:21.590 --> 00:58:24.440
because they want to do recruiting films.

00:58:24.440 --> 00:58:27.420
And I know that some of the older editors,

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:29.160
that's how they got their start,

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:32.603
was editing recruiting
films for second world war.

00:58:34.859 --> 00:58:38.710
But I think you just need
to keep your goal in mind.

00:58:38.710 --> 00:58:41.570
And don't let anybody discourage you

00:58:41.570 --> 00:58:43.563
because I think that part of it is,

00:58:44.450 --> 00:58:48.680
they're just jealous that
they don't have the guts

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:50.823
to do what you wanna do.

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:55.883
And that happened, like
in the reunion where,

00:58:56.730 --> 00:59:00.050
high school reunion where
one of the most popular boys

00:59:00.050 --> 00:59:02.870
when I was going to school came up to me

00:59:02.870 --> 00:59:04.490
and said to me, he goes, "You know what?

00:59:04.490 --> 00:59:06.340
I'm really jealous of you."

00:59:06.340 --> 00:59:07.490
And I go, "Why?"

00:59:07.490 --> 00:59:09.470
And he goes, "Because you went and did

00:59:09.470 --> 00:59:11.050
what you wanted to do."

00:59:11.050 --> 00:59:16.050
And if I, a woman that grew
up in Vancouver's Chinatown,

00:59:16.470 --> 00:59:19.350
can do what I'm doing now,

00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:21.800
then anybody can do it.

00:59:21.800 --> 00:59:26.020
- And I would say, just always be open,

00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:29.770
always be learning,
continue to meet people

00:59:29.770 --> 00:59:32.423
who are following the
path that you wanna meet,

00:59:33.476 --> 00:59:35.353
and like you said, go for it.

00:59:36.426 --> 00:59:39.923
- And I think, Dody, part
of it too is networking.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:45.590
If you go to school and
you meet a couple people

00:59:45.590 --> 00:59:48.410
you really are in sync with,

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:50.640
well then they'll know two more people

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:52.830
and those people would
know four more people.

00:59:52.830 --> 00:59:57.830
And so I always suggest
that to new members

00:59:58.550 --> 01:00:00.623
in the Editor's Guild,

01:00:01.510 --> 01:00:04.930
when they ask "How do I become an editor?"

01:00:04.930 --> 01:00:06.110
Well, that's what you do,

01:00:06.110 --> 01:00:07.880
is you find all these people.

01:00:07.880 --> 01:00:12.300
That's why people that go
to universities do so well,

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:14.720
like Barry Jenkins and Joy MacMillan,

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:16.930
who used to be my assistant,

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:18.850
they all went to Florida State.

01:00:18.850 --> 01:00:21.730
So there is a group of support,

01:00:21.730 --> 01:00:24.830
so if anybody needs a job,

01:00:24.830 --> 01:00:29.710
they know somebody that
will turn you on to a job.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:31.503
- Right, absolutely.

01:00:32.370 --> 01:00:35.880
- Yeah, just keep your eye on the prize.

01:00:35.880 --> 01:00:37.700
That's what I say.
- Right. Eye on the prize.

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:39.050
Well, Maysie, thank you so much

01:00:39.050 --> 01:00:42.130
for generously sharing your
insights and inspiration

01:00:42.130 --> 01:00:43.550
through your rich stories,

01:00:43.550 --> 01:00:47.770
your diverse experiences,
your deep wisdom.

01:00:47.770 --> 01:00:48.960
And it's really been great

01:00:48.960 --> 01:00:51.110
to get to know you actually better.

01:00:51.110 --> 01:00:54.410
And I applaud that you're
opening up the world

01:00:54.410 --> 01:00:56.250
of film editing to so many people,

01:00:56.250 --> 01:00:58.420
and especially to our students in Kenya.

01:00:58.420 --> 01:00:59.520
Thank you so much.

01:00:59.520 --> 01:01:01.140
Thank you for participating

01:01:01.140 --> 01:01:03.930
in the Academy's Visiting
Artist Film Series,

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:06.453
FilmAid Edition for Film Editing.

